            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Italian for plausible, then the Italian for the precondition."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: purché",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "purché — \"provided that, as long as\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puoi uscire, purché tu sia a casa prima di mezzanotte. You can go out, as long as you are home before midnight."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Always followed by the subjunctive: purché tu sia, purché lui venga."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "purché is built from pure \"simply, only\", from Latin pūrē \"purely\" (Lewis & Short), on pūrus \"clean, pure\", and che \"that\": \"only that\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
